// spec: §1.7.3 std §2.15.2
// Methods and calls other languages have: Python's `.format()` and
// `print(a, b)`, JS's `.length`, indexing a string by position. Each
// help is the rok form, from the code as written.
// expect-error: 16:9: no method `format` found for type `str`
// expect-error: help: use an f-string: `f"hello {name}, {n}"`
// expect-error: 17:9: struct `List<i32>` has no field `length`
// expect-error: help: the length is a method: `xs.len()`
// expect-error: 18:15: cannot index into a value of type `str`
// expect-error: help: a `str` is UTF-8 text, not an array of characters; take one with `name.chars().nth(0)`
// expect-error: 19:1: `println` takes 1 argument but 2 were supplied
// expect-error: help: `println` takes one string; put the values in an f-string: `println(f"x = {n}")`
let name = "ada";
let n = 3;
let xs = [1, 2];
let s = "hello {}, {}".format(name, n);
let k = xs.length;
let c: char = name[0];
println("x =", n);
